  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60f727de",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Statements and Strings\n",
    "\n",
    "Let us explore the syntax of Python while starting with a print statement"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "c726e1b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello world from Python!\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Hello world from Python!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d536092e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The print function was given a **string** to process. A string is a sequence of characters. A **character** can be an alphabetic (A through Z, lower and upper case), numeric (any of the digits), white space (spaces, tabs, newlines, etc), syntactic directives (comma, colon, quotation, exclamation, etc), and so forth. A string is just a sequence of the character and typically indicated by surrounding the characters in double-quotes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Standard output is discussed in the [Section Linux](#linux).\n",
    "\n",
    "So, what happened when you pressed Enter? The interactive Python program read the line `print (\"Hello world from Python!\")`, split it into the print statement and the `\"Hello world from Python!\"` string, and then executed the line, showing you the output.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Comments\n",
    "\n",
    "Comments in Python are followed by a `#`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "6e92b96f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# This is a comment"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a0becdb0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Variables\n",
    "\n",
    "You can store data into a **variable** to access it later. For instance:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "60318ff2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello world from Python!\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "hello = 'Hello world from Python!'\n",
    "print(hello)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "439318b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Simple Data Types\n",
    "\n",
    "### Booleans\n",
    "\n",
    "A **boolean** is a value that can have the values `True` or `False`. You can combine booleans with **boolean operators** such as `and` and `or`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "id": "a5117902",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "True\n",
      "False\n",
      "False\n",
      "True\n",
      "True\n",
      "False\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(True and True) # True\n",
    "print(True and False) # False\n",
    "print(False and False) # False\n",
    "print(True or True) # True\n",
    "print(True or False) # True\n",
    "print(False or False) # False"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d56b886",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Numbers\n",
    "\n",
    "The interactive interpreter can also be used as a calculator. For instance, say we wanted to compute a multiple of 21:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "1c99c8ab",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "42\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(21 * 2) # 42"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a7cee4af",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We saw here the print statement again. We passed in the result of the operation 21 \\* 2. An **integer** (or **int**) in Python is a numeric value without a fractional component (those are called **floating point** numbers, or **float** for short).\n",
    "\n",
    "The mathematical operators compute the related mathematical operation to the provided numbers. Some operators are:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Function       |     |\n",
    "|----------|----------------|-----|\n",
    "| \\*       | multiplication |     |\n",
    "| /        | division       |     |\n",
    "| \\+       | addition       |     |\n",
    "| \\-       | subtraction    |     |\n",
    "| \\*\\*     | exponent       |     |\n",
    "\n",
    "Exponentiation $x^y$ is written as x\\*\\*y is x to the yth power.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can combine **float**s and **int**s:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "0d8c9b17",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "13.989090909090908\n",
      "8\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(3.14 * 42 / 11 + 4 - 2) # 13.9890909091\n",
    "print(2**3) # 8"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ab2de8f2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Note that **operator precedence** is important. Using parenthesis to indicate affect the order of operations gives a difference results, as expected:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "e6461929",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "13.98909090909091\n",
      "6.2\n",
      "-0.6\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(3.14 * (42 / 11) + 4 - 2) # 11.42\n",
    "print(1 + 2 * 3 - 4 / 5.0) # 6.2\n",
    "print( (1 + 2) * (3 - 4) / 5.0 ) # -0.6"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "93676233",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Module Management\n",
    "\n",
    "A module allows you to logically organize your Python code. Grouping related code into a module makes the code easier to understand and use. A module is a Python object with arbitrarily named attributes that you can bind and reference. A module is a file consisting of Python code. A module can define functions, classes, and variables. A module can also include runnable code.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Import Statement\n",
    "\n",
    "When the interpreter encounters an import statement, it imports the module if the module is present in the search path. A search path is a list of directories that the interpreter searches before importing a module. The from...import Statement Python's from statement lets you import specific attributes from a module into the current namespace. It is preferred to use for each import its own line such as:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "4f4b9caf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy\n",
    "import matplotlib"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "44dfc736",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "When the interpreter encounters an import statement, it imports the module if the module is present in the search path. A search path is a list of directories that the interpreter searches before importing a module.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The from ... import Statement\n",
    "\n",
    "Python's from statement lets you import specific attributes from a module into the current namespace. The from ... import has the following syntax:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "8952c8de",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from datetime import datetime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "e36f032c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from datetime import datetime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f091e6da",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Date Time in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "The `datetime` module supplies classes for manipulating dates and times in both simple and complex ways. While date and time arithmetic is supported, the focus of the implementation is on efficient attribute extraction for output formatting and manipulation. For related functionality, see also the time and calendar modules.\n",
    "\n",
    "The import Statement You can use any Python source file as a module by executing an import statement in some other Python source file."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "c95ab368",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from datetime import datetime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b2aaba39",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This module offers a generic date/time string parser which is able to parse most known formats to represent a date and/or time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "e545cf59",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from dateutil.parser import parse"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e270b6b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "pandas is an open-source Python library for data analysis that needs to be imported."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "id": "5df16c1e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd"
   ]
  },
  {
