    "pd.to_datetime(df['dates'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2469aa18",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Control Statements\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "Computer programs do not only execute instructions. Occasionally, a choice needs to be made. Such as a choice is based on a condition. Python has several conditional operators:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Function     |     |\n",
    "|----------|--------------|-----|\n",
    "| \\>       | greater than |     |\n",
    "| \\<       | smaller than |     |\n",
    "| ==       | equals       |     |\n",
    "| !=       | is not       |     |\n",
    "\n",
    "Conditions are always combined with variables. A program can make a choice using the if keyword. For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "x = int(input(\"Guess x:\"))\n",
    "if x == 4:\n",
    "   print('Correct!')\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "In this example, *You guessed correctly!* will only be printed if the variable x equals four. Python can also execute multiple conditions using the `elif` and `else` keywords.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "x = int(input(\"Guess x:\"))\n",
    "if x == 4:\n",
    "    print('Correct!')\n",
    "elif abs(4 - x) == 1:\n",
    "    print('Wrong, but close!')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print('Wrong, way off!')\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Iteration\n",
    "\n",
    "To repeat code, the `for` keyword can be used. For example, to display the numbers from 1 to 3, we could write something like this:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "id": "eba3938c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello!\n",
      "Hello!\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for i in range(1, 3):\n",
    "   print('Hello!')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4cf0a918",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The second argument to the `range`, *3*, is not inclusive, meaning that the loop will only get to *10* before it finishes. Python itself starts counting from 0, so this code will also work:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "id": "c3216df7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1\n",
      "2\n",
      "3\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for i in range(0, 3):\n",
    "   print(i + 1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6734a669",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In fact, the range function defaults to starting value of *0*, so it is equivalent to:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 33,
   "id": "7661cc06",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1\n",
      "2\n",
      "3\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for i in range(3):\n",
    "   print(i + 1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "98ab8538",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can also nest loops inside each other:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 34,
   "id": "af299262",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0   0\n",
      "0   1\n",
      "0   2\n",
      "1   0\n",
      "1   1\n",
      "1   2\n",
      "2   0\n",
      "2   1\n",
      "2   2\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for i in range(0,3):\n",
    "    for j in range(0,3):\n",
    "        print(i,' ',j)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0210684a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this case, we have two nested loops. The code will iterate over the entire coordinate range (0,0) to (2,2)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Advanced Datatypes\n",
    "\n",
    "### Lists\n",
    "\n",
    "Lists in Python are ordered sequences of elements, where each element can be accessed using a 0-based index.\n",
    "\n",
    "To define a list, you simply list its elements between square brackets \\[ \\]:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "id": "7470481f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "computers = [\n",
    "  'Workstation-01',\n",
    "  'Server-Alpha',\n",
    "  'Laptop-Pro',\n",
    "  'Backup-Unit',\n",
    "  'Gateway-02']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3a0663f1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To access the first element of the list use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "id": "179e68f7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "'Workstation-01'"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 118,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1547e9ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To access the third element of the list use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 37,
   "id": "b199544e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "'Laptop-Pro'"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 119,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers[2]    "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "608819ed",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You can also use a negative index if you want to start counting elements from the end of the list. Thus, the last element has index -1, the second before the last element has index -2, and so on:\n",
    "\n",
    "To access the last element of the list use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 38,
   "id": "97501696",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "'Gateway-02'"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 120,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers[-1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c14f16a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To access the second last element of the list use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 39,
   "id": "a08d03f3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "'Backup-Unit'"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 121,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers[-2]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b340107d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Python also allows you to take whole slices of the list by specifying a beginning and end of the slice separated by a colon:\n",
    "\n",
    "To access the middle elements, excluding first and last use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 40,
   "id": "dedd898c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "['Server-Alpha', 'Laptop-Pro', 'Backup-Unit']"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 122,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers[1:-1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a56bd19c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As you can see from the example, the starting index in the slice is inclusive and the ending one, exclusive.\n",
    "\n",
    "Python provides a variety of methods for manipulating the members of a list.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can add elements with append:\n",
    "\n",
    "To adds an element to the end use"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 41,
   "id": "b23c7798",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "computers.append('Laptop-Pro')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9edd80c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As you can see, the elements in a list need not be unique.\n",
    "\n",
    "Merge two lists with extend:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 42,
   "id": "bda736e3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "computers.extend(['Node-05', 'Node-06'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "911e48f0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Find the index of the first occurrence of an element with index:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 43,
   "id": "9df0ecd1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "2"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 125,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers.index('Laptop-Pro') "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "851a911e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Remove elements by value with remove:\n",
    "\n",
    "To remove only the first occurrence of the value"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 44,
   "id": "6c277504",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "computers.remove('Gateway-02')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4eee00d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Remove elements by index with pop:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 45,
   "id": "2ff5290d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "['Workstation-01',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Backup-Unit',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Node-05',\n",
       " 'Node-06']"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 127,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers.pop(1)\n",
    "computers"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "36e2d4d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Notice that pop returns the element being removed, while remove does not.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you are familiar with stacks from other programming languages, you can use insert and pop:\n",
    "\n",
    "Insert 'Mainframe' at the very beginning:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 46,
   "id": "e1f8961c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "['Mainframe',\n",
       " 'Workstation-01',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Backup-Unit',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Node-05',\n",
       " 'Node-06']"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 128,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers.insert(0, 'Mainframe')\n",
    "computers"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5ac61327",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To pop() without an index removes the last element:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 47,
   "id": "8010fc84",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "['Mainframe',\n",
       " 'Workstation-01',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Backup-Unit',\n",
       " 'Laptop-Pro',\n",
       " 'Node-05']"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 129,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers.pop()\n",
    "computers"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "517bc345",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The Python documentation contains a [full list of list operations]().\n",
    "\n",
    "To go back to the range function you used earlier, it simply creates a list of numbers:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 48,
   "id": "e7341acb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "range(0, 10)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 130,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "range(10)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 49,
   "id": "f60f4093",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "range(2, 10, 2)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 131,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "range(2, 10, 2)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "91ce5944",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Hostlists\n",
    "\n",
    "When managing **Cloud Computing** clusters or **High-Performance Computing (HPC)** environments, you often need to address groups of machines. Instead of listing `server01, server02, server03...` manually, we use **Hostlists**.\n",
    "\n",
    "A Hostlist is a compressed representation of network hosts, commonly used in job schedulers (like Slurm) and cluster management tools.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Understanding the Syntax\n",
    "\n",
    "Hostlists use **bracket expansion** to group ranges of numbers or letters.\n",
    "\n",
    "|                         |                                        |\n",
    "|-------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n",
    "| **Compressed Hostlist** | **Expanded Result**                    |\n",
    "| `server[01-03]`         | `server01, server02, server03`         |\n",
    "| `node[1,5,10-12]`       | `node1, node5, node10, node11, node12` |\n",
    "| `gpu-[a-c]`             | `gpu-a, gpu-b, gpu-c`                  |\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Using Hostlists in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "The most robust way to handle these in Python is via the `python-hostlist` library. It allows you to expand strings into lists and compress lists back into strings. Hostlist can be installed with\n",
    "\n",
    "``` bash\n",
    "pip install python-hostlist\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Basic Operations"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 50,
   "id": "82592ad0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import hostlist"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3b9c2a38",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 1. Expand a hostlist string into a Python list"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 51,
   "id": "hostlist-expansion",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "['node01', 'node02', 'node03']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| label: hostlist-expansion\n",
    "#| echo: true\n",
    "#| output: true\n",
    "\n",
    "import hostlist\n",
    "hosts = hostlist.expand_hostlist(\"node[01-03]\")\n",
    "print(hosts) "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 52,
   "id": "0b0b98e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "['red01-compute', 'red02-compute', 'red03-compute', 'red05-compute']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import hostlist\n",
    "hosts = hostlist.expand_hostlist(\"red[01-03,05]-compute\")\n",
    "print(hosts)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c22036d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 2. Compress a list of hosts into a hostlist string"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 53,
   "id": "686ed165",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "node[10-12,15]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import hostlist\n",
    "my_nodes = ['node10', 'node11', 'node12', 'node15']\n",
    "compressed = hostlist.collect_hostlist(my_nodes)\n",
    "print(compressed)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "00aa0278",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 3. Practical Cloud/AI Use Case: GPU Clusters\n",
    "\n",
    "In AI training, you might need to run a distributed training job across specific GPU nodes. Using hostlists prevents configuration errors.Pythondef"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 54,
   "id": "fa8a01f4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Initializing training on 4 nodes...\n",
      " -> Connecting to gpu-cluster-001\n",
      " -> Connecting to gpu-cluster-002\n",
      " -> Connecting to gpu-cluster-003\n",
      " -> Connecting to gpu-cluster-004\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import hostlist\n",
    "def setup_distributed_training(host_string):\n",
    "    nodes = hostlist.expand_hostlist(host_string)\n",
    "    print(f\"Initializing training on {len(nodes)} nodes...\")\n",
    "    for node in nodes:\n",
    "        # Logic to trigger ssh or container execution\n",
    "        print(f\" -> Connecting to {node}\")\n",
    "setup_distributed_training(\"gpu-cluster-[001-004]\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1e52f97d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 4. Key Functions Reference\n",
    "\n",
    "expand_hostlist(string): Returns a list of individual hostnames. collect_hostlist(list): Returns the shortest possible bracket-syntax string. find_common_prefix(list): Useful for identifying the cluster name from a list of nodes.5. Common PitfallsPadding: node\\[1-3\\] yields node1, node2, node3, while node\\[01-03\\] yields node01, node02, node03. Ensure your script matches your DNS/Inventory naming.Non-Sequential Nodes: If your cluster has \"holes\" (e.g., node 4 is down), use commas: node\\[1-3,5-10\\].\n",
    "\n",
    "### Sets\n",
    "\n",
    "Python lists can contain duplicates, as you saw previously. We see that the name Lapto-Pro occus twice. To just use unique names, we can use a set."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 55,
   "id": "006ad6ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "{'Workstation-01', 'Server-Alpha', 'Laptop-Pro', 'Backup-Unit'}\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computers = [\n",
    "  'Workstation-01', 'Server-Alpha', 'Laptop-Pro', \n",
    "  'Backup-Unit', 'Laptop-Pro', 'Workstation-01'\n",
    "]\n",
    "unique_computers = set(computers)\n",
    "print(unique_computers)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1e9990bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Keep in mind that a *set* is an **unordered** collection of objects; therefore, we cannot access them by index:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "unique_computers[0]\n",
    "# Traceback (most recent call last):\n",
    "#   File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\n",
    "#   TypeError: 'set' object does not support indexing\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "However, we can convert a set back to a list easily:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 56,
   "id": "8974d707",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Workstation-01\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "unique_list = list(unique_computers)\n",
    "# ['Backup-Unit', 'Server-Alpha', 'Workstation-01', 'Laptop-Pro']\n",
    "\n",
    "print(unique_list[0])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eb0a66a7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Notice that in this case, the order of elements in the new list matches the order in which the elements were displayed when the set was created. However, **you should not assume this is the case in general**. Do not make any assumptions about the order of elements in a set when it is converted to any type of sequential data structure.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Set Operations\n",
    "\n",
    "You can change a set's contents using the `add`, `remove`, and `update` methods, which correspond to the `append`, `remove`, and `extend` methods in a list.\n",
    "\n",
    "In addition to these, *set* objects support the operations you may be familiar with from mathematical sets: **union**, **intersection**, and **difference**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 57,
   "id": "5e798014",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Union: All unique computers across both offices:\n",
      "\t {'Workstation-01', 'Server-Alpha', 'Laptop-Pro'}\n",
      "Intersection: Computers present in both offices:\n",
      "\t {'Server-Alpha'}\n",
      "Difference: Computers in office_a but NOT in office_b:\n",
      "\t {'Workstation-01'}\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "office_a = {'Workstation-01', 'Server-Alpha'}\n",
    "office_b = {'Server-Alpha', 'Laptop-Pro'}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Union: All unique computers across both offices:\\n\\t\", office_a | office_b)\n",
    "print (\"Intersection: Computers present in both offices:\\n\\t\", office_a & office_b)\n",
    "print (\"Difference: Computers in office_a but NOT in office_b:\\n\\t\", office_a - office_b)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d5cb366",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You can read more about these in the [Python documentation for sets](https://www.google.com/search?q=https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html%23set).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Removal and Testing for Membership in Sets\n",
    "\n",
    "One important advantage of a `set` over a `list` is that **access to elements is fast**. If you are familiar with different data structures from a Computer Science class, the Python list is implemented by an array, while the set is implemented by a hash table.\n",
    "\n",
    "We will demonstrate this with an example. Let us say we have a list and a set of the same number of elements (approximately 100 thousand):\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "import sys, random, timeit\n",
    "nums_set = set([random.randint(0, sys.maxvalue) for _ in range(10**5)])\n",
    "nums_list = list(nums_set)\n",
    "len(nums_set)\n",
    "# 100000\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use the [timeit](https://docs.python.org/2/library/timeit.html) Python module to time 100 operations that test for the existence of a member in either the list or set:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "import timeit\n",
    "timeit.timeit('random.randint(0, sys.maxint) in nums',\n",
    "              setup='import random; nums=%s' % str(nums_set), number=100)\n",
    "# 0.0004038810729980469\n",
    "timeit.timeit('random.randint(0, sys.maxint) in nums',\n",
    "              setup='import random; nums=%s' % str(nums_list), number=100)\n",
    "# 0.398054122924804\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The exact duration of the operations on your system will be different, but the takeaway will be the same: searching for an element in a set is orders of magnitude faster than in a list. This is important to keep in mind when you work with large amounts of data.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Dictionaries\n",
    "\n",
    "One of the very important data structures in python is a dictionary also referred to as `dict`.\n",
    "\n",
    "A dictionary represents a key value store:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 58,
   "id": "82343682",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "computer['name']:  mycomputer\n",
      "computer['memory']:  16\n",
      "mycomputer 16\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computer = {\n",
    "  'name': 'mycomputer',\n",
    "  'memory': 16,\n",
    "  'kind': 'Laptop'\n",
    "  }\n",
    "print(\"computer['name']: \", computer['name'])\n",
    "print(\"computer['memory']: \", computer['memory'])\n",
    "\n",
    "# A convenient for to print by named attributes is\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"{name} {memory}\".format(**computer))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "243c0d63",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This form of printing with the format statement and a reference to data increases the readability of the print statements.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can delete elements with the following commands:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 59,
   "id": "30e3b835",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "del computer['name'] # remove entry with key 'name'\n",
    "# computer\n",
    "# {'Age': 100, 'Class': 'Scientist'}\n",
    "computer.clear()     # remove all entries in dict\n",
    "# computer\n",
    "# {}\n",
    "del computer         # delete entire dictionary\n",
    "# computer\n",
    "# Traceback (most recent call last):\n",
    "#  File \"<stdin>\", line 1, in <module>\n",
    "#  NameError: name 'computer' is not defined"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f3d45dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You can iterate over a dict:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 60,
   "id": "3123bce1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "name mycomputer\n",
      "memory 16\n",
      "kind Laptop\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computer = {\n",
    "  'name': 'mycomputer',\n",
    "  'memory': 16,\n",
    "  'kind': 'Laptop'\n",
    "  }\n",
    "for item in computer:\n",
    "  print(item, computer[item])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c1ad40c1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Dictionary Keys and Values\n",
    "\n",
    "You can retrieve both the keys and values of a dictionary using the keys() and values() methods of the dictionary, respectively:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 61,
   "id": "318fe2cd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "dict_keys(['name', 'memory', 'kind'])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 143,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computer.keys()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 62,
   "id": "feef0ba9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "dict_values(['mycomputer', 16, 'Laptop'])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 144,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "computer.values() "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "68d32c3e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Both methods return lists. Please remember howver that the keys and order in which the elements are returned are not necessarily the same. It is important to keep this in mind:\n",
    "\n",
    "::: callout-warning\n",
    "*You cannot make any assumptions about the order in which the elements of a dictionary will be returned by the keys() and values() methods*.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, you can use ordered_dict that preserves the order\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "However, you can assume that if you call `keys()` and `values()` in sequence, the order of elements will at least correspond in both methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Counting with Dictionaries\n",
    "\n",
    "One application of dictionaries that frequently comes up is counting the elements in a sequence. For example, say we have a sequence of coin flips:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 63,
   "id": "4dc9f0ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "['tails', 'heads', 'heads', 'heads', 'heads', 'tails', 'tails', 'tails', 'tails', 'tails']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import random\n",
    "die_rolls = [\n",
    "  random.choice(['heads', 'tails']) for _ in range(10)\n",
    "]\n",
    "print(die_rolls)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1646522f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The actual list die_rolls will likely be different when you execute this on your computer since the outcomes of the die rolls are random.\n",
    "\n",
    "To compute the probabilities of heads and tails, we could count how many heads and tails we have in the list:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 64,
   "id": "9d7a7967",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Probability of heads: 0.40\n",
      "Probability of tails: 0.60\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "counts = {'heads': 0, 'tails': 0}\n",
    "for outcome in die_rolls:\n",
    "   assert outcome in counts\n",
    "   counts[outcome] += 1\n",
    "print('Probability of heads: %.2f' % (counts['heads'] / len(die_rolls)))\n",
    "print('Probability of tails: %.2f' % (counts['tails'] / sum(counts.values())))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "12d5f402",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In addition to how we use the dictionary counts to count the elements of coin_flips, notice a couple of things about this example:\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  We used the assert outcome in the `count` statement. The assert statement in Python allows you to easily insert debugging statements in your code to help you discover errors more quickly. assert statements are executed whenever the internal Python `__debug__` variable is set to True, which is always the case unless you start Python with the -O option which allows you to run *optimized* Python.\n",
    "\n",
    "2.  When we computed the probability of tails, we used the built-in `sum` function, which allowed us to quickly find the total number of coin flips. The `sum` is one of many built-in functions you can [read about here](https://docs.python.org/2/library/functions.html).\n",
    "\n",
    "## Functions\n",
    "\n",
    "You can reuse code by putting it inside a function that you can call in other parts of your programs. Functions are also a good way of grouping code that logically belongs together in one coherent whole. A function has a unique name in the program. Once you call a function, it will execute its body which consists of one or more lines of code:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 65,
   "id": "ef5223a1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "gpu-node-01: True\n",
      "GPU-node-02: False\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "names = [\"gpu-node-01\", \"GPU-node-02\"]\n",
    "def is_valid_node_name(name):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Checks if a cloud node name is lowercase and within length limits.\"\"\"\n",
    "    is_lowercase = name.islower()\n",
    "    correct_length = 3 <= len(name) <= 63\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return is_lowercase and correct_length\n",
    "\n",
    "# Testing the function\n",
    "for name in names:\n",
    "    print(f\"{name}:\", is_valid_node_name(name))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1c9188cb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The def keyword tells Python we are defining a function. As part of the definition, we have the function name, check_triangle, and the parameters of the function -- variables that will be populated when the function is called.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Classes\n",
    "\n",
    "A class is an encapsulation of data and the processes that work on them. The data is represented in member variables, and the processes are defined in the methods of the class (methods are functions inside the class). For example, let's see how to define a class for a computer:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 66,
   "id": "600e28db",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Node ai-processor-01 is now running.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "class CloudNode:\n",
    "    \"\"\"Represents a virtual machine in a cloud cluster.\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __init__(self, name, cpu_cores, ram_gb):\n",
    "        # Encapsulation and Validation\n",
    "        if not self._is_valid_config(cpu_cores, ram_gb):\n",
    "            print(f\"Invalid configuration for node: {name}\")\n",
    "            return\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Member Variables (Data)\n",
    "        self.name = name\n",
    "        self.cpu_cores = cpu_cores\n",
    "        self.ram_gb = ram_gb\n",
    "        self.status = \"stopped\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    def _is_valid_config(self, cpu, ram):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Internal process to validate hardware specs.\"\"\"\n",
    "        # Rules: Must have at least 1 core and 2GB RAM\n",
    "        return cpu >= 1 and ram >= 2\n",
    "\n",
    "    def start(self):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Method to change the state of the object.\"\"\"\n",
    "        self.status = \"running\"\n",
    "        print(f\"Node {self.name} is now {self.status}.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Creating an instance (Object)\n",
    "my_node = CloudNode(\"ai-processor-01\", 8, 32)\n",
    "my_node.start()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e4f325ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Commandline Programs\n",
    "\n",
    "In the world of Cloud Computing and AI, automation is key. Instead of using a graphical interface to click buttons, which is not scalable, we write scripts that can be triggered from a terminal (command line). These scripts allow us to manage hundreds of servers or trigger massive AI training jobs with a single command. They can be integrated in shell scripts as well as called from other languages or frameworks\n",
    "\n",
    "### The `docopt`Advantage\n",
    "\n",
    "A popular way to create command line interfaces in Python is using a library called `docopt`. Unlike other libraries where you have to write complex code to define your flags and arguments, `docopt` follows a unique philosophy: **the documentation is the code.** You simply write a standard help message (a \"docstring\") at the top of your file, and `docopt` automatically:\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **Parses** the arguments you type in the terminal.\n",
    "\n",
    "2.  **Validates** that the user provided the correct number of inputs.\n",
    "\n",
    "3.  **Generates** a help menu automatically when the user types `--help`.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Anatomy of a Cloud Script\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following program, we define a \"Cloud Quota\" checker. This is a common task in enterprise environments where we must ensure a user isn't trying to spin up a Virtual Machine that is too large for their budget or service tier.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use three key components:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The Docstring:** Defines the \"Usage\" pattern.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The Logic Function:** A reusable block of code (`is_valid_quota`) that can be imported into other programs.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The Main Guard:** The `if __name__ == '__main__':` block, which ensures the script only runs its CLI logic when executed directly, not when imported as a library.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "\"\"\"Usage: check_quota.py [-h] CPU RAM STORAGE\n",
    "\n",
    "Check if a Cloud Node configuration fits within the Free Tier quota.\n",
    "\n",
    "Arguments:\n",
    "  CPU        Number of CPU cores.\n",
    "  RAM        Amount of RAM in GB.\n",
    "  STORAGE    Disk storage in GB.\n",
    "\n",
    "Options:\n",
    "  -h --help  Show this screen.\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "from docopt import docopt\n",
    "\n",
    "def is_valid_quota(cpu, ram, storage):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Business Logic: \n",
    "    Max 4 Cores, Max 16GB RAM, Max 100GB Storage.\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    return (cpu <= 4 and \n",
    "            ram <= 16 and \n",
    "            storage <= 100)\n",
    "\n",
    "if __name__ == '__main__':\n",
    "    # docopt parses the docstring above to create the CLI\n",
    "    arguments = docopt(__doc__)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Extract and convert arguments\n",
    "    c = int(arguments['CPU'])\n",
    "    r = int(arguments['RAM'])\n",
    "    s = int(arguments['STORAGE'])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    valid = is_valid_quota(c, r, s)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Modern f-string formatting (preferred over % operator)\n",
    "    print(f\"Node config (CPU:{c}, RAM:{r}GB, Disk:{s}GB) valid: {valid}\")\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Here is an example on how to use it.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` bash\n",
    "python check_quota.py 2 8 50\n",
    "# Output: Node config (CPU:2, RAM:8GB, Disk:50GB) valid: True\n",
    "\n",
    "python check_quota.py 8 32 500\n",
    "# Output: Node config (CPU:8, RAM:32GB, Disk:500GB) valid: False\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Lambda Expressions {#s-python-lambda}\n",
    "\n",
    "As opposed to normal functions in Python which are defined using the `def` keyword, lambda functions in Python are anonymous functions that do not have a name and are defined using the `lambda` keyword. The generic syntax of a lambda function is in the form of `lambda arguments: expression`, as shown in the following example:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 67,
   "id": "d069ce5f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello Albert!\n",
      "None\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "greeter = lambda x: print('Hello %s!'%x)\n",
    "print(greeter('Albert'))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b24195be",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now consider the following examples:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 68,
   "id": "4538972c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "power2 = lambda x: x ** 2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "65b05d29",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The `power2` function defined in the expression, is equivalent to the following definition:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 69,
   "id": "9ac75730",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def power2(x):\n",
    "    return x ** 2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4b9a86c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Lambda functions are useful when you need a function for a short period. \n",
    "\n",
    "Why use Lambdas in Cloud & Data Science?\n",
    "In cloud monitoring or data processing, you often use lambdas to transform data on the fly. For example, if you have a list of server memory capacities in GB and need to convert them to MB for a report:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 70,
   "id": "41afd074",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[8192, 16384, 32768, 65536]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "memory_gb = [8, 16, 32, 64]\n",
    "# Use map with a lambda to multiply each element by 1024\n",
    "memory_mb = list(map(lambda x: x * 1024, memory_gb))\n",
    "print(memory_mb)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "38cecc5e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In Python, the filter function returns a filter object or the iterator which gets lazily evaluated which means neither we can access the elements of the filter object with index nor we can use `len()` to find the length of the filter object."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 71,
   "id": "bd0999de",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[2, 4]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "list_a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "filter_obj = filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, list_a)\n",
    "# Convert the filer obj to a list\n",
    "even_num = list(filter_obj)\n",
    "print(even_num)\n",
    "# Output: [2, 4]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "df2385a7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The filter() function combined with a lambda expression is a powerful way to process data. To make this more readable and \"Pythonic,\" you can often replace it with a List Comprehension, which is generally preferred in modern Python for its speed and clarity.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the Pythonic \"List Comprehension\" is the standard way to filter lists in Python. It reads almost like plain English: \"Give me x for every x in list_a if x is even.\" THis illustrates, although python as many other languages have many advanced features, often the most straight forward are better, and in this case even faster."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 72,
   "id": "d188a01f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[2, 4]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "Python\n",
    "list_a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "\n",
    "# More readable and usually faster than filter()\n",
    "even_num = [x for x in list_a if x % 2 == 0]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(even_num) "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ccdb9eb6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In Python, we can have a small usually a single linear anonymous function called Lambda function which can have any number of arguments just like a normal function but with only one expression with no return statement. The result of this expression can be applied to a value.\n",
    "\n",
    "Basic Syntax:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 73,
   "id": "9591588a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<function __main__.<lambda>(arguments)>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 155,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "lambda arguments : expression"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ccc4bea6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For example, a function in python\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "def multiply(a, b):\n",
    "   return a*b\n",
    "\n",
    "#call the function\n",
    "multiply(3*5) #outputs: 15\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The same function can be written as Lambda function. This function named as multiply is having 2 arguments and returns their multiplication.\n",
    "\n",
    "Lambda equivalent for this function would be:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "multiply = Lambda a, b : a*b\n",
    "\n",
    "print(multiply(3, 5))\n",
    "# outputs: 15\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Here a and b are the 2 arguments and a\\*b is the expression whose value is returned as an output.\n",
    "\n",
    "Also, we don't need to assign the Lambda function to a variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "(lambda a, b : a*b)(3*5)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Lambda functions are mostly passed as a parameter to a function which expects a function objects like in map or filter.\n",
    "\n",
    "### map {#s-python-map}\n",
    "\n",
    "The basic syntax of the map function is\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "map(function_object, iterable1, iterable2,...)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "map functions expect a function object and any number of iterable like a list or dictionary. It executes the function_object for each element in the sequence and returns a list of the elements modified by the function object.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "def multiply(x):\n",
    "   return x * 2\n",
    "\n",
    "map(multiply2, [2, 4, 6, 8])\n",
    "# Output [4, 8, 12, 16]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "If we want to write the same function using Lambda\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "map(lambda x: x*2, [2, 4, 6, 8])\n",
    "# Output [4, 8, 12, 16]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### dictionary\n",
    "\n",
    "Now, let us see how we can iterate over a dictionary using map and lambda Let us say we have a dictionary object"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 74,
   "id": "6e408585",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dict_movies = [\n",
    "    {'movie': 'avengers', 'comic': 'marvel'},\n",
    "    {'movie': 'superman', 'comic': 'dc'}]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "79854466",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can iterate over this dictionary and read the elements of it using map and lambda functions in following way:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 75,
   "id": "acb0b1ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<map at 0x10e7518d0>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 157,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "map(lambda x : x['movie'], dict_movies)  # Output: ['avengers', 'superman']\n",
    "map(lambda x : x['comic'],  dict_movies)  # Output: ['marvel', 'dc']\n",
    "map(lambda x : x['movie'] == \"avengers\", dict_movies)\n",
    "# Output: [True, False]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ed362089",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In Python3, map function returns an iterator or map object which gets lazily evaluated which means neither we can access the elements of the map object with index nor we can use len() to find the length of the map object. We can force convert the map output i.e. the map object to list as shown next:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "map_output = map(lambda x: x*2, [1, 2, 3, 4])\n",
    "print(map_output)\n",
    "# Output: map object: <map object at 0x04D6BAB0>\n",
    "list_map_output = list(map_output)\n",
    "print(list_map_output) # Output: [2, 4, 6, 8]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Iterators\n",
    "\n",
    "In Python, an iterator protocol is defined using two methods: `__iter()__` and `next()`. The former returns the iterator object and latter returns the next element of a sequence. Some advantages of iterators are as follows:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Readability\n",
    "- Supports sequences of infinite length\n",
    "- Saving resources\n",
    "\n",
    "There are several built-in objects in Python which implement iterator protocol, e.g. string, list, dictionary. In the following example, we create a new class that follows the iterator protocol. We then use the class to generate `log2` of numbers:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 76,
   "id": "2633fa6d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0.0\n",
      "1.0\n",
      "1.584962500721156\n",
      "2.0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from math import log2\n",
    "\n",
    "class LogTwo:\n",
    "    \"Implements an iterator of log two\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __init__(self,last = 0):\n",
    "        self.last = last\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __iter__(self):\n",
    "        self.current_num = 1\n",
    "        return self\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __next__(self):\n",
    "        if self.current_num <= self.last:\n",
    "            result = log2(self.current_num)\n",
    "            self.current_num += 1\n",
    "            return result\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            raise StopIteration\n",
    "\n",
    "L = LogTwo(5)\n",
    "i = iter(L)\n",
    "print(next(i))\n",
    "print(next(i))\n",
    "print(next(i))\n",
    "print(next(i))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3e284eb3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As you can see, we first create an instance of the class and assign its `__iter()__` function to a variable called `i`. Then by calling the `next()` function four times, we get the following output:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` bash\n",
    "$ python iterator.py\n",
    "0.0\n",
    "1.0\n",
    "1.584962500721156\n",
    "2.0\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "As you probably noticed, the lines are `log2()` of 1, 2, 3, 4 respectively.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Generators\n",
    "\n",
    "Before we go to Generators, please understand Iterators. Generators are also Iterators but they can only be iterated over once. That is because generators do not store the values in memory instead they generate the values on the go. If we want to print those values then we can either simply iterate over them or use the for loop.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Generators with function\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, we have a function named as multiplyBy10 which prints all the input numbers multiplied by 10.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "def multiplyBy10(numbers):\n",
    "   result = []\n",
    "   for i in numbers:\n",
    "      result.append(i*10)\n",
    "   return result\n",
    "\n",
    "new_numbers = multiplyBy10([1,2,3,4,5])\n",
    "\n",
    "print new_numbers  #Output: [10, 20, 30, 40 ,50]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Now, if we want to use Generators here then we will make the following changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "def multiplyBy10(numbers):\n",
    "   for i in numbers:\n",
    "      yield(i*10)\n",
    "\n",
    "new_numbers = multiplyBy10([1,2,3,4,5])\n",
    "\n",
    "print new_numbers  #Output: Generators object\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "In Generators, we use yield() function in place of return(). So when we try to print new_numbers list now, it just prints Generators object. The reason for this is because Generators do not hold any value in memory, it yields one result at a time. So essentially it is just waiting for us to ask for the next result. To print the next result we can just say print next(new_numbers) , so how it is working is its reading the first value and squaring it and yielding out value 1. Also in this case, we can just print next(new_numbers) 5 times to print all numbers and if we do it for the 6th time then we will get an error StopIteration which means Generators has exhausted its limit and it has no 6th element to print.\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "print next(new_numbers)  #Output: 1\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Generators using for loop\n",
    "\n",
    "If we now want to print the complete list of squared values then we can just do:\n",
    "\n",
    "``` python\n",
    "def multiplyBy10(numbers):\n",
    "   for i in numbers:\n",
    "      yield(i*10)\n",
    "\n",
    "new_numbers = multiplyBy10([1,2,3,4,5])\n",
    "\n",
    "for num in new_numbers:\n",
    "   print num\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The output will be:\n",
    "\n",
    "```         \n",
    "10\n",
    "20\n",
    "30\n",
    "40\n",
    "50\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Generators with List Comprehension\n",
    "\n",
    "Python has something called List Comprehension, if we use this then we can replace the complete function def with just:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 77,
   "id": "09227b66",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "new_numbers = [x*10 for x in [1,2,3,4,5]]\n",
    "print (new_numbers)  #Output: [10, 20, 30, 40 ,50]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "342de892",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Here the point to note is square brackets \\[\\] in line 1 is very important. If we change it to () then again we will start getting Generators object."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 78,
   "id": "435dd992",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "<generator object <genexpr> at 0x10b374ee0>\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "new_numbers = (x*10 for x in [1,2,3,4,5])\n",
    "print (new_numbers)  #Output: Generators object"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "945a9a4a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can get the individual elements again from Generators if we do a for loop over `new_numbers`, as we did previously. Alternatively, we can convert it into a list and then print it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 79,
   "id": "3c12d78b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "new_numbers = (x*10 for x in [1,2,3,4,5])\n",
    "print (list(new_numbers))  #Output: [10, 20, 30, 40 ,50]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "189ea286",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "But here if we convert this into a list then we lose on performance, which we will just see next.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why use Generators?\n",
    "\n",
    "Generators are better with Performance because it does not hold the values in memory and here with the small examples we provide it is not a big deal since we are dealing with a small amount of data but just consider a scenario where the records are in millions of data set. And if we try to convert millions of data elements into a list then that will make an impact on memory and performance because everything will in memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Let us see an example of how Generators help in Performance. First, without Generators, normal function taking 1 million records and returns the result for 1 million entries."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 80,
   "id": "memory-profile-test",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Memory Baseline: 176.27 MB\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Memory After Loading 100k Nodes: 206.92 MB\n",
      "Actual RAM consumed by List: 30.66 MB\n",
      "Inventory generation took: 0.1245 seconds\n",
      "\n",
      "Sample Node Data:\n",
      "{'node_id': 'i-dddc2718', 'hostname': 'node-000000', 'type': 'g4dn.xlarge', 'region': 'ap-southeast-1', 'status': 'stopped', 'cpu_cores': 64}\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| label: memory-profile-test\n",
    "#| echo: true\n",
    "#| error: true\n",
    "\n",
    "import random\n",
    "import time\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import psutil\n",
    "\n",
    "# Configuration for our Cloud Cluster\n",
    "regions = ['us-east-1', 'us-west-2', 'eu-central-1', 'ap-southeast-1']\n",
    "node_types = ['t3.medium', 'm5.large', 'g4dn.xlarge', 'p4d.24xlarge']\n",
    "statuses = ['running', 'stopped', 'terminated', 'provisioning']\n",
    "\n",
    "def get_memory_usage():\n",
    "    \"\"\"Returns current memory usage in MB.\"\"\"\n",
    "    process = psutil.Process(os.getpid())\n",
    "    return process.memory_info().rss / 1024 / 1024\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Record Initial State\n",
    "mem_before = get_memory_usage()\n",
    "print(f'Memory Baseline: {mem_before:.2f} MB')\n",
    "\n",
    "def generate_inventory(num_nodes):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Creates a list of cloud node dictionaries.\"\"\"\n",
    "    inventory = []\n",
    "    for i in range(num_nodes):\n",
    "        node = {\n",
    "            'node_id': f\"i-{random.getrandbits(32):x}\", # Simulated AWS Instance ID\n",
    "            'hostname': f\"node-{i:06d}\",\n",
    "            'type': random.choice(node_types),\n",
    "            'region': random.choice(regions),\n",
    "            'status': random.choice(statuses),\n",
    "            'cpu_cores': random.choice([2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64])\n",
    "        }\n",
    "        inventory.append(node)\n",
    "    return inventory\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Execute and Time the Generation\n",
    "t1 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "cloud_inventory = generate_inventory(100000) \n",
    "t2 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Record Final State\n",
    "mem_after = get_memory_usage()\n",
    "mem_used = mem_after - mem_before\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f'Memory After Loading 100k Nodes: {mem_after:.2f} MB')\n",
    "print(f'Actual RAM consumed by List: {mem_used:.2f} MB')\n",
    "print(f'Inventory generation took: {t2-t1:.4f} seconds')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example: Peek at the first node\n",
    "print(\"\\nSample Node Data:\")\n",
    "print(cloud_inventory[0])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c86de04",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let us see an example of how Generators help in Performance. First, without Generators, normal function taking 1 million records and returns the result\\[people\\] for 1 million."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 81,
   "id": "86be9865",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Memory (Before): 206.95 Mb\n",
      "Memory (After): 228.33 Mb\n",
      "Took 0.0454 seconds\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| label: memory-profile-test\n",
    "#| echo: true\n",
    "#| error: true\n",
    "\n",
    "import random\n",
    "import time\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import psutil  # Standard for memory monitoring\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Fixed typos: 'random' was misspelled as 'randon'\n",
    "names = ['John', 'Jack', 'Adam', 'Steve', 'Rick']\n",
    "majors = ['Math', 'CompScience', 'Arts', 'Business', 'Economics']\n",
    "\n",
    "def get_memory():\n",
    "    process = psutil.Process(os.getpid())\n",
    "    return process.memory_info().rss / 1024 / 1024  # Convert to Mb\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Record initial memory\n",
    "mem_before = get_memory()\n",
    "print(f'Memory (Before): {mem_before:.2f} Mb')\n",
    "\n",
    "def people_list(num_people):\n",
    "   result = []\n",
    "   for i in range(num_people):\n",
    "      person = {\n",
    "            'id' : i,\n",
    "            'name' : random.choice(names),\n",
    "            'major' : random.choice(majors) # Fixed typo here\n",
    "            }\n",
    "      result.append(person)\n",
    "   return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Fixed time.clock() -> time.perf_counter() \n",
    "# time.clock() was removed in Python 3.8\n",
    "t1 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "# Reduced count slightly for faster rendering, adjust as needed\n",
    "people = people_list(100000) \n",
    "t2 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Record final memory\n",
    "mem_after = get_memory()\n",
    "print(f'Memory (After): {mem_after:.2f} Mb')\n",
    "print(f'Took {t2-t1:.4f} seconds')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c80b458",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "I am just giving approximate values to compare it with the next execution but we just try to run it we will see a serious consumption of memory with a good amount of time taken."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 82,
   "id": "generator-memory-profile",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Memory (Before): 228.36 Mb\n",
      "Memory (After): 228.36 Mb\n",
      "Took 0.000017 seconds (Generator creation only)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| label: generator-memory-profile\n",
    "#| echo: true\n",
    "#| error: true\n",
    "\n",
    "import random\n",
    "import time\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import psutil\n",
    "\n",
    "names = ['John', 'Jack', 'Adam', 'Steve', 'Rick']\n",
    "majors = ['Math', 'CompScience', 'Arts', 'Business', 'Economics']\n",
    "\n",
    "def get_memory():\n",
    "    process = psutil.Process(os.getpid())\n",
    "    return process.memory_info().rss / 1024 / 1024\n",
    "\n",
    "# Initial Memory\n",
    "mem_before = get_memory()\n",
    "print(f'Memory (Before): {mem_before:.2f} Mb')\n",
    "\n",
    "def people_generator(num_people):\n",
    "    # In Python 3, 'range' is the same as Python 2's 'xrange'\n",
    "    for i in range(num_people):\n",
    "        person = {\n",
    "            'id': i,\n",
    "            'name': random.choice(names),\n",
    "            'major': random.choice(majors) # Fixed typo 'randon'\n",
    "        }\n",
    "        yield person\n",
    "\n",
    "# Timer using Python 3 compatible function\n",
    "t1 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "\n",
    "# This creates the generator object but DOES NOT run the loop\n",
    "people_gen = people_generator(10000000)\n",
    "\n",
    "# To actually see memory/time impact, we must consume the generator\n",
    "# Example: Convert to list (CAUTION: this will spike memory)\n",
    "# people_list = list(people_gen) \n",
    "\n",
    "t2 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Final Memory\n",
    "mem_after = get_memory()\n",
    "print(f'Memory (After): {mem_after:.2f} Mb')\n",
    "print(f'Took {t2-t1:.6f} seconds (Generator creation only)')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5d6c69cd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now after running the same code using Generators, we will see a significant amount of performance boost with almost 0 Seconds. And the reason behind this is that in the case of Generators, we do not keep anything in memory so the system just reads 1 at a time and yields that."
   ]
  }
